    {
     "type": "quiz",
     "question": "Why is eastward jet lag (e.g., NYC to London) harder than westward jet lag (NYC to LA)?",
     "options": [
      {
       "text": "Eastward flights are longer",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "The human circadian clock naturally drifts to slightly more than 24 hours, so it can delay (westward) more easily than advance (eastward)",
       "correct": true
      },
      {
       "text": "Eastward destinations have more sunlight",
       "correct": false
      },
      {
       "text": "Westward travelers sleep on the plane",
       "correct": false
      }
     ],
     "explanation": "The human SCN, isolated in cave studies (Aschoff, Siffre), runs at ~24h 15min — slightly longer than 24 hours. This means our internal clock can naturally delay (going to bed later) more easily than advance (going to bed earlier). Westward travel asks for delay (easier). Eastward asks for advance (harder). Rule: ~1 day recovery per time zone east, ~0.5 day per zone west.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Quick Check</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#a0a0b0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Why Eastward Travel Is Harder</text><circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"500\" r=\"200\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"500\" font-size=\"28\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">SCN Clock</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"540\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">~24h 15min</text><line x1=\"350\" y1=\"500\" x2=\"200\" y2=\"500\" stroke=\"#10b981\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow1)\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"475\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Delay</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"530\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(westward — easy)</text><line x1=\"750\" y1=\"500\" x2=\"900\" y2=\"500\" stroke=\"#ef4444\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow2)\"/><text x=\"820\" y=\"475\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Advance</text><text x=\"820\" y=\"530\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#ef4444\" text-anchor=\"middle\">(eastward — hard)</text><defs><marker id=\"arrow1\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"0\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><polygon points=\"0 0, 10 3, 0 6\" fill=\"#10b981\"/></marker><marker id=\"arrow2\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"10\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><polygon points=\"0 0, 10 3, 0 6\" fill=\"#ef4444\"/></marker></defs><text x=\"550\" y=\"800\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The clock leans WEST</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"840\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Drifting later (delay) is easier than waking earlier (advance).</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"900\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">East: ~1 day recovery per zone. West: ~0.5 day per zone.</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"980\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ec4899\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Walker: \"It's why we say 'sleep on it' — we delay decisions overnight.\"</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Natural drift favors delay over advance."
     }
    },
    {
     "type": "concept",
     "title": "Lesson 2 Synthesis",
     "content": "You now understand the two-process model: Process C (circadian, SCN-driven, light-anchored, melatonin-mediated) tells your body WHEN. Process S (adenosine accumulation, ATP byproduct, caffeine-blockable) tells your body HOW MUCH. Sleep occurs when both signals align with darkness. Disruption of either — by jet lag, shift work, screens, or caffeine — disrupts sleep architecture. Next lesson: what sleep actually IS at the brain-wave level.",
     "visual": {
      "type": "diagram",
      "svg": "<svg viewBox=\"0 0 1100 1100\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><rect width=\"1100\" height=\"1100\" fill=\"#1a1a2e\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"36\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lesson 2 Synthesis</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"115\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#a0a0b0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Two Forces, One Outcome</text><rect x=\"60\" y=\"200\" width=\"450\" height=\"250\" fill=\"#6366f1\" opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#6366f1\" stroke-width=\"3\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"285\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#6366f1\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">PROCESS C</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Circadian Rhythm</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"325\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• SCN master clock</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"355\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Light → melanopsin → reset</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"385\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Melatonin: darkness signal</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"415\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Answers: WHEN to sleep</text><rect x=\"590\" y=\"200\" width=\"450\" height=\"250\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" opacity=\"0.2\" stroke=\"#8b5cf6\" stroke-width=\"3\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"815\" y=\"245\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#8b5cf6\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">PROCESS S</text><text x=\"815\" y=\"285\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sleep Pressure</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"325\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Adenosine from ATP</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"355\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Builds while awake</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"385\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Caffeine blocks A1 receptors</text><text x=\"610\" y=\"415\" font-size=\"18\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Answers: HOW MUCH sleep</text><rect x=\"200\" y=\"500\" width=\"700\" height=\"160\" fill=\"#ffd700\" opacity=\"0.15\" stroke=\"#ffd700\" stroke-width=\"3\" rx=\"10\"/><text x=\"550\" y=\"555\" font-size=\"26\" fill=\"#ffd700\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">When C is low AND S is high</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"595\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">= You sleep</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"625\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#a0a0b0\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Both must be aligned for restorative sleep</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"730\" font-size=\"24\" fill=\"#10b981\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Causes of Disruption</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"780\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Screens at night → suppress melatonin → delay C</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"815\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Late caffeine → mask S → fragment depth</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"850\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Jet lag → C desynchronizes from environment</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"885\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Shift work → both C and S misaligned</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"920\" font-size=\"20\" fill=\"#ffffff\">• Alcohol → fragments S clearance, blocks REM</text><text x=\"550\" y=\"1010\" font-size=\"22\" fill=\"#ec4899\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Next: Sleep Architecture — NREM, REM, and the 90-minute cycle.</text></svg>",
      "caption": "Mastering C and S explains every sleep problem."
     }
    }
