     "explanation": "Morning light — especially bright outdoor light (1,000+ lux even on cloudy days) — is the most potent zeitgeber (time-giver) for the circadian clock. Melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells (most sensitive to ~480 nm blue wavelengths) signal the SCN directly, anchoring the day's circadian rhythm. Walker recommends 30+ minutes within the first hour of waking. Indoor lighting (300 lux) is too dim for this purpose.",
