     "title": "A Reader's Checklist for Evaluating Pop-Science Health Claims",
     "content": "The Why We Sleep controversy is best used as a template for a portable skill: reading any popular science health book with informed skepticism rather than either uncritical acceptance or blanket dismissal. A practical checklist follows. First, trace dramatic-sounding statistics back to their original source before repeating them, since a compelling number that cannot be traced to a specific, checkable study is a warning sign rather than a footnote detail. Second, separate correlational language from causal language; a book stating that short sleepers show higher rates of a disease is describing an association, while a claim that short sleep directly causes that disease requires a different, harder kind of evidence, typically controlled experimental or longitudinal designs. Third, look for effect sizes and confidence intervals rather than headline numbers alone, since a statistically significant finding can still describe a small, practically modest effect that a dramatic retelling makes sound larger than it is. Fourth, weigh single studies against systematic reviews and meta-analyses; a single striking result is far less reliable than a conclusion replicated across many independent studies and research groups. Fifth, and most importantly, judge a book's core thesis separately from its illustrative flourishes, since an author can be broadly right about the big picture while still getting specific supporting details wrong, exactly the pattern seen in this case. Applied to Why We Sleep itself, this checklist supports a balanced verdict: the book's central argument, that chronic insufficient sleep meaningfully harms health, cognition, and safety, remains well supported by a broad, independent scientific literature, even though several of its specific illustrative statistics required correction after public scrutiny, a genuinely healthy outcome for how popular science should work.",
